WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
Two threat models, and who is responsible.

2
00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:05.357
The specification is candid about its limits.

3
00:00:05.637 --> 00:00:20.379
It says MCP enables arbitrary data access and code execution paths, that tools represent arbitrary code execution and must be treated with caution, and that the protocol cannot enforce its security principles itself.

4
00:00:20.659 --> 00:00:29.555
Those principles are user consent and control, data privacy, and tool safety, and implementers are asked to build them into their applications.

5
00:00:29.835 --> 00:00:36.085
So security in MCP lives in three places, and you will often be responsible for more than one.

6
00:00:36.365 --> 00:00:41.208
Servers must validate inputs, control access, and avoid leaking what they touch.

7
00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:48.144
Hosts and clients must get consent, show users what is happening, and keep a human able to deny tool calls.

8
00:00:48.424 --> 00:00:58.280
And the people who install servers, which includes you every time you run claude mcp add, decide what code runs on their machine and with which credentials.

9
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.589
The MCP docs split the threats into two models.

10
00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:10.531
The protocol threat model covers remote servers: OAuth flows, token handling, confused deputies, and server-side request forgery.

11
00:01:10.811 --> 00:01:28.453
The local threat model covers your machine: a stdio server is an ordinary process launched under your user account, which inherits your environment variables and can read whatever you can read, including SSH keys and cloud credentials, and can open network connections anywhere.

12
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:33.896
None of that is governed by the protocol; it is governed by how you run the process.

13
00:01:34.176 --> 00:01:38.891
This lesson goes through both, first as a server author and then as an installer.

14
00:01:39.171 --> 00:01:45.229
Week 4 widens the view to agent permissions, sandboxing, and injection defenses in general.
